<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Literature, Hanoi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1070</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Văn Miếu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Literature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> was founded in 1070 as a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s first national academy. From 1484 the names of successful doctoral candidates were carved on stone stelae resting on tortoises; 82 stelae survive, covering examinations from 1442 to 1779, and are listed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-literature-hanoi.jpg" alt="Temple of Literature, Hanoi" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Chuoibk at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanoi_Temple_of_Literature_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The names of Lê-era doctoral graduates were carved on stone stelae resting on turtles at Hanoi's <b>Temple of Literature</b> from 1484; dozens of these stelae still stand there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
